--- !u!114 &1920307469
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 1920307464}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: f4688fdb7df04437aeb418b961361dc5, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 1
  m_RaycastPadding: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: "The Monkey and the Turtle\r\n\r\nOne day, a Monkey met a Turtle on the
    road and asked, \"Where are you going?\"\r\n\r\n\"I am going to find something
    to eat, for I have had no food for three whole days,\" said the Turtle.\r\n\r\n\"I,
    too, am hungry,\" said the Monkey, \"and since we are both hungry, let us go\r\ntogether
    and hunt food for our stomach's sake.\"\r\n\r\nThey soon became good friends
    and chatted along the way, so that the time passed quickly. Before they had gone
    far, the Monkey saw a large bunch of yellow bananas on a tree at a distance.
    \r\n\r\n\"Oh, what a good sight that is!\" he cried. \"Don't you see the bananas
    hanging on that banana tree?\u201D He pointed with his first finger toward the
    tree, \u201CThey are fine! I can taste them already.\"\r\n\r\nBut the Turtle
    was short-sighted and could not see them. After a while, they came near the tree,
    and then he finally saw them. The two friends were very glad. The mere sight
    of the ripe, yellow fruit made them hungrier.\r\n\r\nBut the Turtle could not
    climb the tree, so he agreed that the Monkey should go up alone and throw some
    of the fruit down to him. \r\n\r\nThe Monkey was up in a flash and, seating himself
    comfortably, he began to eat the ripest of the fruit, and forgot to drop any
    down to the Turtle waiting below.\r\n\r\nThe Turtle called for some, but the
    Monkey pretended not to hear. He ate even the peelings and refused to drop a
    bit to his friend, who was patiently begging under the tree.\r\n\r\nAt last,
    the Turtle became angry, very angry indeed. So, he thought he would take revenge.
    \r\n\r\nWhile the Monkey was having a good time filling his stomach, the Turtle
    gathered sharp, broken pieces of glass and stuck them, one by one, all around
    the banana tree. Then he hid himself under a coconut shell not far away.\r\n\r\nThe
    Monkey could not eat all the bananas, for there was enough to last a good-sized
    family for several days. He ate all that he could, and then he started coming
    down the tree with great difficulty. \r\n\r\nThe sharp glasses then cut the hand
    of the Monkey, wounding him. He had a hard time, and his hands were full of cuts
    in many places. The Turtle was satisfied with his revenge.\r\n\r\nBut now, the
    Monkey was very angry at the trick the Turtle had played on him. He began searching
    for the Turtle, planning to punish him. For a while, he could not find his former
    friend. \r\n\r\nTired and in pain, he sat down on a coconut shell nearby. His
    anger only grew stronger as he rested, thinking about what the Turtle had done.\r\n\r\nHe
    sat there for a long time, sore from his wounds and wondering where the Turtle
    could be. As he shifted his weight on the shell, the Turtle\u2014who was hiding
    inside\u2014made a small noise.\r\n\r\nThe Monkey heard it and looked around,
    confused. He couldn\u2019t tell where the sound had come from. At last, he lifted
    the shell\u2014and there was the Turtle!\r\n\r\n\u201CAh! So here you are!\u201D
    the Monkey shouted. \u201CSay your prayers. This is the end for you!\u201D\r\n\r\nHe
    grabbed the Turtle by the neck and carried him to the riverbank. There, he prepared
    to punish him. He built a big fire and brought out a mortar and pestle. He was
    deciding whether to burn the Turtle or grind him into pieces.\r\n\r\nWhen everything
    was ready, he asked the Turtle to choose how he wanted to die: in the fire or
    in the mortar. The Turtle begged for his life. But when he saw that the Monkey
    would not forgive him, he cried, \u201CPlease, do anything\u2014but don\u2019t
    throw me into the water!\u201D\r\n\r\nWhen the Monkey heard this, he thought
    it was the perfect punishment. He picked up the Turtle with his bleeding fingers
    and threw him as hard as he could into the river.\r\n\r\nTo the Monkey\u2019s
    surprise, the Turtle was delighted. He laughed and popped his head out of the
    water.\r\n\r\n\u201CThis is my home!\u201D the Turtle shouted. \u201CThe water
    is where I belong!\u201D\r\n\r\nHearing this, the Monkey became so angry that
    he lost control. He jumped into the river to catch the Turtle\u2014but he could
    not swim, and he drowned.\r\n\r\nSince that day, monkeys and turtles have been
    bitter enemies.\r\n\r\n- The End -\r\n"
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
